What each check proves:

* `TestDeleteInvalidatesLearned` — builds an `a -> x -> y -> !b` chain, learns a clause under `{a,b}`, deletes the chain, and asserts the *same* query is now SAT. This is the stale-learned-clause regression.
* `TestRandomDifferential` / `TestMutationChaos` — differential tests against a brute-force oracle over every assignment. `TestMutationChaos` interleaves `AddClause`, `DeleteClause`, `EliminateVar`, and assumption queries and, for every UNSAT answer, checks that the returned core is (a) a subset of the assumptions, (b) UNSAT, and (c) irredundant.
* `TestMinimumCoreExact` / `TestMinimumCoreCardinality` — compare against the true minimum computed by exhaustive subset enumeration, including disjoint and mixed-size cores.
* `TestVariableElimination` — checks BVE is equisatisfiable against the original oracle, including reintroduction/defrost.
* `TestReuseAcrossCalls` — learned clauses persist across many queries.

A wider stress run (2 400 churn iterations over 6 seeds, with core minimality checks) also passes; the suite runs in well under a second.

### Near-linear-overhead notes

The expensive work is done once per clause mutation (`DeleteClause`, `EliminateVar` rebuild the level-0 closure and watch lists) and once per query (level-0 replay). Search itself reuses the learned database, so a sequence of queries on a stable formula pays only for the level-0 replay plus whatever new search is actually required, rather than a fresh solve. Mutation invalidation is trace-based, so it evicts only clauses that genuinely depended on the mutated clause.
